    "reasoning": "All 16 final answers agree with the mathematics and their own captured MA solutions. One worked-solution error occurs in q-129241: its first cross-product line substitutes ⟨1,-2,1⟩ and ⟨1,-1,0⟩, whose cross product is ⟨1,1,1⟩. The subsequent determinant correctly uses the actual normals and gives ⟨4,2,1⟩, so the final answer remains correct. Individually: q-128400 uses normal ⟨7,1,0⟩ and point (0,3,0). q-70833 has normal ⟨-3,2,0⟩×⟨0,0,1⟩=⟨2,3,0⟩; substituting P gives 2x+3y=4. q-140504: every matrix has matching entries across its main diagonal. q-72492: C repeats leading column 2 in its first two rows; A and B have strictly advancing leading columns and bottom zero rows. q-129217: A repeats leading column 5, while B and C satisfy the echelon conditions. q-42662: counting grid squares gives u=(-2,0), v=(2,4), w=(4,-8), hence μ=-2 and λ=-4. q-102791: substituting y=ue^{2x} leaves e^{2x}u''=0. q-90524: substituting y=ut^3 leaves t^4(tu''+3u')=0. q-77666: reduction gives tu''+u'=0, so u=ln t provides the independent solution t^3 ln t. q-65000: reduction gives xu''-u'=0, so u=x^2 provides x^4. q-129174: the normals' cross product is ⟨0,-2,2⟩. q-129171: it is ⟨4,15,10⟩. q-129215: setting z=0 gives (x,y)=(0,3), and the normals' cross product is ⟨6,6,4⟩. q-129224: setting z=0 gives (x,y)=(4,0), and the normals' cross product is ⟨9,3,5⟩. q-129241 and q-129258 have nonzero normal cross products ⟨4,2,1⟩ and ⟨4,6,13⟩ respectively; both pairs intersect in a line, making III alone true. No submitted-grade evidence is supplied; all MA answer evidence below comes from the captured worked solutions.",
